				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2000367&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608045290" alt="WrongJohnSilver" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2000367)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/wrongjohnsilver" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2000367); return false;" >WrongJohnSilver</a></span> <span class="odate time_1471028089 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">12 Aug 2016 18:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2557907">
									

<p>When I read and critiqued the draft, one of the things I pointed out was that the tale didn't depict the struggle of the hard-boiled detective in the Foundation, but the struggle of noir tropes in the Foundationverse. The lampshading made it key that it's not the director lashing out at the detective, but the story lashing out at the genre.</p>
<p>As a result, the items that made it cringey on one level made it eye-opening for me, on a different level. It doesn't mean it's necessarily effective with that reading for everyone, but that was my take.</p>

							</div>

								
					
